<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Greek People's Liberation Army</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1942">1942</a>–<a href="/years/1945">1945</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as ELAS</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Greek <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-liberation-army">People's Liberation Army</a> (ELAS) was the military wing of EAM and the largest Greek resistance force during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a> occupation. Formed in 1942, with Aris Velouchiotis as its leading figure and Stefanos Sarafis as military commander, it fought the occupiers and rival resistance groups. After clashes with British and government forces in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> in December 1944, it was disbanded under the Varkiza Agreement of February 1945.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/greek-peoples-liberation-army.jpg" alt="Greek People's Liberation Army" width="320" height="245" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Uploader: Υπουργείο Εξωτερικών Photographer: Sjt. Johnson (see [3]) · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%CE%91%CE%BD%CF%84%CE%AC%CF%81%CF%84%CE%B5%CF%82_%CF%84%CE%BF%CF%85_%CE%95%CE%91%CE%9C-%CE%95%CE%9B%CE%91%CE%A3_(restored).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By 1944 the left-wing resistance movement EAM and its army <b>ELAS</b> controlled much of mountainous Greece.</blockquote><a href="/state/political-committee-of-national-liberation">Political Committee of National Liberation</a></li></ul></section>
